FILE: examples/modal/index.html, examples/modal/README.md
FEATURE: modal-stacking
DATE: 2026-08-03
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'modal-displayer-verification'

================================================================================
CHANGE 1 — index.html: nested triggers and a second modal
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
        <div id="loginModal" class="demo-modal">
            <h2>Sign in</h2>
            <input type="text" placeholder="Username">
            <input type="password" placeholder="Password">
            <button type="button">Sign in</button>
            <button type="button" class="modal-close">Cancel</button>
        </div>

NEW_SECTION
        <div id="loginModal" class="demo-modal">
            <h2>Sign in</h2>
            <input type="text" placeholder="Username">
            <input type="password" placeholder="Password">
            <button type="button">Sign in</button>
            <button type="button" class="modal-close">Cancel</button>
            <hr>
            <!-- triggers inside a displayed modal stack a new layer on top of it -->
            <button type="button" class="open-modal open-nested" data-form="nestedModal">Forgot password?</button>
            <button type="button" class="open-modal open-self" data-form="loginModal">Open myself (ignored)</button>
        </div>

        <div id="nestedModal" class="demo-modal">
            <h2>Nested: reset password</h2>
            <p>Opened from inside the sign-in modal — the modal underneath stays open.</p>
            <input type="email" placeholder="Email">
            <button type="button" class="modal-close">Back</button>
        </div>

REASON
The example is both the documentation demo and the fixture the automated browser
suite drives, so stacking needed a realistic case in it. "Forgot password?" inside
the sign-in modal is the canonical reason to nest, and it exercises the whole path:
a trigger living inside a displayed modal's cloned content, opening a second layer
while the first stays intact.

The "Open myself" trigger demonstrates the self-stacking guard. Without the guard
this is the input that would blank the modal's source a second time and lose its
content permanently, so having it visible in the demo documents the refusal as
intended behaviour rather than an oversight.

No CSS was needed for the second modal; it reuses the .demo-modal class, which
confirms that a stacked layer needs no special styling from the consumer.

================================================================================
CHANGE 2 — README.md: stacking and the new API members
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
- Trigger-driven opening (`config.className` + `config.formIdAttribute`).
- Closing by close button, `Escape`, and overlay click.
...
The instance is exposed as `window.demoModal`, so you can try `demoModal.close()`, `demoModal.mainForm` and `demoModal.displayForm` from the browser console.

NEW_SECTION
- Trigger-driven opening (`config.className` + `config.formIdAttribute`).
- **Stacking** — "Forgot password?" inside the sign-in modal opens a second layer over it; `Escape` pops one layer at a time. "Open myself" shows that a modal cannot be stacked on itself.
- Closing by close button, `Escape`, and overlay click.
...
The instance is exposed as `window.demoModal`, so you can try `demoModal.close()`, `demoModal.closeAll()`, `demoModal.depth`, `demoModal.mainForm` and `demoModal.displayForm` from the browser console.

REASON
Describes what the two new buttons demonstrate, and adds the members introduced by
this change to the list of things worth trying from the console — depth in particular
is the quickest way to see the stack behaving.
